{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Security deposit transfer duty and withholding penalties","requirement":"A new owner who takes over a rented property must obtain the tenant's security deposit and accrued interest, invest it, give all required notices, pay interest and return the deposit. Willfully withholding a deposit from a tenant on State or federal assistance draws a civil penalty.","key_value":"$500–$2,000 civil penalty per offense","coverage_conditions":"Residential landlords and successor owners; the withholding penalty applies where the tenant received State or federal financial or rental assistance.","applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":"Withholding penalty applies where the tenant received State or federal financial or rental assistance.","coverage_quotes":[]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Civil penalty of not less than $500 or not more than $2,000 for each offense for willfully withholding a deposit; unlawful use of deposit monies is a disorderly persons offense with a fine of at least $200 or up to 30 days imprisonment (N.J.S.A. 46:8-25).","effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-21","quoted_span":"The landlord may be liable for a civil penalty of not less than $500 or not more than $2,000 for each offense.","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act good cause requirement","requirement":"A landlord may recover possession of a residential dwelling only by tenant consent or through the legal eviction process, and may not evict or fail to renew a written or oral lease without good cause. A Notice to Quit is required for all good-cause evictions except nonpayment of rent.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential dwelling units; applies to written and oral leases. Each cause except nonpayment of rent must be described in detail in the landlord's written notice.","applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":"Good cause required; Notice to Quit required except for nonpayment of rent; a Notice to Cease may also be required.","coverage_quotes":[]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"No residential landlord may evict or fail to renew a lease, whether it is a written or an oral lease without good cause.","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act rent increase procedure and unconscionability","requirement":"A landlord may not raise rent mid-lease; to increase rent the landlord must terminate the old lease and offer a new lease at the higher rent with proper written notice. An increase must not be unconscionable and must comply with municipal rent-control ordinances.","key_value":"No statutory cap; increase must not be unconscionable","coverage_conditions":"Residential tenants; increases must comply with any municipal ordinance governing rent increases; unconscionability is judged on the facts.","applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":"Applies when a landlord seeks to increase rent; a valid notice to quit and notice of rent increase is required before eviction for nonpayment of the increase.","coverage_quotes":[]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1(f)","quoted_span":"The increase in rent must not be unconscionable; it must not be so unreasonable as to shock the conscience of a fair and honest person and must comply with any municipal ordinances governing rent increases.","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Newly Constructed Multiple Dwellings rent-control exemption","requirement":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings are exempt from any local rent-control ordinance for 30 years after construction is completed. Rents subsidized by government funding may also be exempt from local rent control.","key_value":"30 years after completion of construction","coverage_conditions":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings for 30 years following completion of construction; rents subsidized by governmental funding may also be exempt.","applicability":{"conditions":[{"type":"built_within_years","role":"exempt","years":30,"basis":"construction"},{"type":"other","text":"Rents subsidized by governmental funding may also be exempt from local rent control ordinances"}],"per_tenancy":null,"coverage_quotes":["newly constructed multiple dwellings shall be exempt from any local rent control ordinances for a period of 30 years following completion of construction of the building.","Rents that are subsidized by governmental funding may also be exempt from local rent control ordinances."]},"exemptions":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings for 30 years after construction; government-subsidized rents.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-84.5","quoted_span":"newly constructed multiple dwellings shall be exempt from any local rent control ordinances for a period of 30 years following completion of construction of the building.","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Rent increase ban for condominium or cooperative conversion costs","requirement":"If a building is converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership of units, rents may not be increased to cover costs resulting solely from the conversion. Increases to cover new services or amenities are allowed.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Applies to all tenants in a building converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership, whether or not they qualify as senior citizens or disabled protected tenants.","applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":"Applies when a building is converted to a condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership of units.","coverage_quotes":[]},"exemptions":"Rent increases to cover the cost of new services or amenities.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31","quoted_span":"If a building is converted to a condominium or cooperative form of ownership, or to fee simple ownership of units, rents may not be increased to cover cos ts resulting solely from the conversion (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31).","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
